<section>
  <exercises>
    <heading level="1">Section 2.3 Exercises</heading>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, find the volume generated when the region between the two curves is rotated around the given axis. Use both the shell method and the washer method. Use technology to graph the functions and draw a typical slice by hand.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="114" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] Bounded by the curves $$y = 3x,x = 0,$$ and $$y = 3$$ rotated around the $$y \text{-axis} .$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="115" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] Bounded by the curves $$y = 3x,y = 0, \text{and} x = 3$$ rotated around the $$y \text{-axis} .$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$54\pi 54\pi$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="116" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] Bounded by the curves $$y = 3x,y = 0, \text{and} y = 3$$ rotated around the $$x \text{-axis} .$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="117" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] Bounded by the curves $$y = 3x,y = 0, \text{and} x = 3$$ rotated around the $$x \text{-axis} .$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$81\pi 81\pi$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="118" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] Bounded by the curves $$y = 2x^{3},y = 0, \text{and} x = 2$$ rotated around the $$y \text{-axis} .$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="119" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] Bounded by the curves $$y = 2x^{3},y = 0, \text{and} x = 2$$ rotated around the $$x \text{-axis} .$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$512\pi 7512\pi 7$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, use shells to find the volumes of the given solids. Note that the rotated regions lie between the curve and the $$x \text{-axis}$$ and are rotated around the $$y \text{-axis} .$$</paragraph>
    <exercise number="120" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = 1 - x^{2},x = 0, \text{and} x = 1$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="121" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = 5x^{3},x = 0, \text{and} x = 1$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$2\pi 2\pi$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="122" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = \frac{1}{x},x = 1, \text{and} x = 100$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="123" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = \sqrt{1 - x^{2}},x = 0, \text{and} x = 1$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$2\pi 32\pi 3$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="124" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = \frac{1}{1 + x^{2}},x = 0, \text{and} x = 3$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="125" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = \sin x^{2},x = 0, \text{and} x = \sqrt{\pi}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$2\pi 2\pi$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="126" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1 - x^{2}}},x = 0, \text{and} x = \frac{1}{2}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="127" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = \sqrt{x},x = 0, \text{and} x = 1$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$4\pi 54\pi 5$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="128" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = \left( {1 + x^{2}} \right)^{3},x = 0, \text{and} x = 1$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="129" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = 5x^{3} - 2x^{4},x = 0, \text{and} x = 2$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$64\pi 364\pi 3$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, use shells to find the volume generated by rotating the regions between the given curve and $$y = 0$$ around the $$x \text{-axis} .$$</paragraph>
    <exercise number="130" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = \sqrt{1 - x^{2}},x = 0, x = 1$$ and the x-axis</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="131" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = x^{2},x = 0, x = 2$$ and the x-axis</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$32\pi 532\pi 5$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="132" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = \frac{x^{3}}{2}, x = 0, x = 2,$$ and the x-axis</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="133" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = \frac{2}{x^{2}}, x = 1, x = 2,$$ and the x-axis</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$7 \pi 6 7 \pi 6$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="134" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$x = \frac{1}{1 + y^{2}},y = 4$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="135" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$x = \frac{1 + y^{2}}{y},y = 1, y = 4,$$ and the y-axis</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$48 \pi 48 \pi$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="136" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$x = \sqrt{4 - y^{2}},x = 0,y = 0$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="137" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$x = y^{3} - 2y^{2}, x = 0, x = 9$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$308 \pi 5 308 \pi 5$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="138" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$x = \sqrt{y} + 1, x = 1, x = 3,$$ and the x-axis</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="139" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$x = \sqrt[3]{27y} \text{and} x = \frac{3y}{4}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$512 \pi 7 512 \pi 7$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, find the volume generated when the region between the curves is rotated around the given axis.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="140" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = 3 - x,y = 0,x = 0, \text{and} x = 2$$ rotated around the $$y \text{-axis} .$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="141" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = x^{3},x = 0, \text{and} y = 8$$ rotated around the $$y \text{-axis} .$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$8 \pi 5 8 \pi 5$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="142" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = x^{2},y = x,$$ rotated around the $$y \text{-axis} .$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="143" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = \sqrt{x},y = 0, \text{and} x = 1$$ rotated around the line $$x = 2.$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$28\pi 1528\pi 15$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="144" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = \frac{1}{4 - x},x = 1, x = 2 \text{and} y = 0$$ rotated around the line $$x = 4.$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="145" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = \sqrt{x} \text{and} y = x^{2}$$ rotated around the $$y \text{-axis} .$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$3\pi 103\pi 10$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="146" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$y = \sqrt{x} \text{and} y = x^{2}$$ rotated around the line $$x = 2.$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="147" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$x = y^{3},x = \frac{1}{y},x = 1, \text{and} x = 2$$ rotated around the $$x \text{-axis} .$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$\pi 6\cdot 22/35-1110=\pi 1012\cdot 22/3-11\approx 2.5286\pi 6\cdot 22/35-1110=\pi 1012\cdot 22/3-11\approx 2.5286$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="148" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$x = y^{2} \text{and} y = x$$ rotated around the line $$y = 2.$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="149" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] Left of $$x = \sin \left( {\pi y} \right),$$ right of $$y = x,$$ around the $$y \text{-axis} .$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$0.98760.9876$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, use technology to graph the region. Determine which method you think would be easiest to use to calculate the volume generated when the function is rotated around the specified axis. Then, use your chosen method to find the volume.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="150" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] $$y = x^{2}$$ and $$y = 4x$$ rotated around the $$y \text{-axis} .$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="151" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] $$y = \cos \left( {\pi x} \right),y = \sin \left( {\pi x} \right),x = \frac{1}{4}, \text{and} x = \frac{5}{4}$$ rotated around the $$y \text{-axis} .$$ This exercise requires advanced technique. You may use technology to perform the integration.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$3232$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="152" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] $$y = x^{2} - 2x,x = 2, \text{and} x = 4$$ rotated around the $$y \text{-axis} .$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="153" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] $$y = x^{2} - 2x,x = 2, \text{and} x = 4$$ rotated around the $$x \text{-axis} .$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$496\pi 15496\pi 15$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="154" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] $$y = 3x^{3} - 2,y = x, \text{and} x = 2$$ rotated around the $$x \text{-axis} .$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="155" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] $$y = 3x^{3} - 2,y = x, \text{and} x = 2$$ rotated around the $$y \text{-axis} .$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$398\pi 15398\pi 15$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="156" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] $$x = \sin \left( {\pi y^{2}} \right)$$ and $$x = \sqrt{2}y$$ rotated around the $$x \text{-axis} .$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="157" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>[T] $$x = y^{2},x = y^{2} - 2y + 1, \text{and} x = 2$$ rotated around the $$y \text{-axis} .$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$15.907415.9074$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, use the method of shells to approximate the volumes of some common objects, which are pictured in accompanying figures.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="158" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Use the method of shells to find the volume of a sphere of radius $$r.$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="159" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Use the method of shells to find the volume of a cone with radius $$r$$ and height $$h.$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$13\pi r2h13\pi r2h$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="160" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Use the method of shells to find the volume of an ellipsoid $$\left( {x^{2}/a^{2}} \right) + \left( {y^{2}/b^{2}} \right) = 1$$ rotated around the $$x \text{-axis} .$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="161" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Use the method of shells to find the volume of a cylinder with radius $$r$$ and height $$h.$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$\pi r2h\pi r2h$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="162" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Use the method of shells to find the volume of the donut created when the circle $$x^{2} + y^{2} = 4$$ is rotated around the line $$x = 4.$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="163" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Consider the region enclosed by the graphs of $$y = f(x),y = 1 + f(x),x = 0,y = 0,$$ and $$x = a &gt; 0.$$ What is the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated around the $$y \text{-axis} ?$$ Assume that the function is defined over the interval $$\lbrack 0,a\rbrack.$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$\pi a2\pi a2$$ units3</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="164" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Consider the function $$y = f(x),$$ which decreases from $$f(0) = b$$ to $$f(1) = 0.$$ Set up the integrals for determining the volume, using both the shell method and the disk method, of the solid generated when this region, with $$x = 0$$ and $$y = 0,$$ is rotated around the $$y \text{-axis} .$$ Prove that both methods approximate the same volume. Which method is easier to apply? (Hint: Since $$f(x)$$ is one-to-one, there exists an inverse $$f^{-1}(y).)$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</section>
